221
00:32:27,652 --> 00:32:32,652
The hierarchical clusters can also be analyzed under two categories.

222
00:32:32,652 --> 00:32:37,172
One is aglomerative, which is bottom-up.

223
00:32:38,131 --> 00:32:46,071
Aglomerative, basically, it tells you that at the beginning, you treat each point as a cluster.

224
00:32:46,732 --> 00:32:55,491
And then as moving forward from the initial point, you repeatedly combine the two nearest clusters into one.

225
00:32:55,491 --> 00:33:01,831
And then as you keep doing this in an iterative way, you construct such a dendogram.

226
00:33:02,371 --> 00:33:05,751
And then by using the dendogram, you can identify the clusters.

227
00:33:06,731 --> 00:33:11,891
The second methodology is top-down divisive methodology.

228
00:33:12,411 --> 00:33:19,751
So this time, instead of starting from the bottom-up, so this time you start from top-down.

229
00:33:19,751 --> 00:33:34,891
You treat the whole data point as one cluster, and you recursively split the main cluster into subclusters all the way until you end up each point as a cluster.

230
00:33:35,391 --> 00:33:48,252
But usually, the commonly used hierarchical clustering methodology is agglomerative clustering, so we will also focus agglomerative clustering in this course.

